  "raw": {
    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/7RJM7/Experience-Coordinator,2026-25643.html": {
      "title": "Experience Coordinator",
      "description": "Experience Coordinator -\r\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Experience Coordinator Deliver exceptional frontline service and student engagement support, helping students, prospective students and visitors navigate their university journey. Mō te tūka | The role Join our Wheako Tauira | Student Experience team and be at the heart of creating positive experiences for prospective students, current students, whānau, and visitors. Working in our Student and Visitor Centre, you'll help deliver welcoming, responsive, and high-quality support while contributing to initiatives that enhance engagement, recruitment, retention, and student success. Some tasks of the role include: Providing professional frontline support through in-person, phone, email, and digital channels. Responding to, triaging, and resolving student and prospective student enquiries. Supporting student experience and engagement activities across the student journey. Maintaining CRM records and monitoring service requests through to resolution. Updating student communication channels and supporting key communications initiatives. Contributing to knowledge resources, process improvements, and digital service enhancements. This is a varied and people-focused role where you'll combine customer service, administration, communication, and relationship-building skills to support students throughout their Lincoln University journey. Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This fixed term role covers parental leave and concludes in July 2027. Hours of work are Monday – Friday, 8:30 am – 1:00 pm (22.5 hours per week), at our Lincoln Campus. The salary for this position is $59,000 per annum, pro rata to part-time hours. Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have A relevant qualification and/or experience in a customer service, administration, or education environment. Experience using CRM systems (Dynamics experience preferred) and confidence working with multiple digital systems. Strong communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to engage effectively with people from diverse backgrounds. Excellent organisational skills, attention to detail, and the ability to manage competing priorities. The ability to research, analyse, and provide accurate information in response to complex enquiries. Demonstrated initiative, sound judgement, and a proactive approach to problem-solving. Intermediate Microsoft Office skills and a commitment to delivering outstanding customer service. Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to sarah.rees2@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. Apply now – we will review applications as they are received and may close the advertising early. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-25643 Posted on 28 Sep 2026 Closes on 11 Oct 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Administration, Communications, Customer Service, Sales Work type(s) Fixed term part-time More details (document) PD Experience Coordinator September 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 11 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance."
    },
    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/d1NM7/Building-Maintenance-Lead,2026-25659.html": {
      "title": "Building Maintenance Lead",
      "description": "Building Maintenance Lead -\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Building Maintenance Lead Lead planned maintenance and building projects to ensure campus facilities are safe, compliant, well-maintained, and fit for purpose. Mō te tūka | The role Join our Property Services Group team and play a key role in maintaining and enhancing Lincoln University's campus environment. This position is responsible for planning and delivering preventative maintenance programmes, coordinating building-related projects, managing contractors, and ensuring University facilities remain safe, functional, and fit for purpose for staff, students, visitors, and tenants. Some tasks of the role include: Developing and managing planned and preventative maintenance programmes and budgets. Coordinating maintenance and capital projects from scoping through to completion. Engaging and managing contractors to ensure work is delivered safely, on time, and to a high standard. Monitoring project costs, expenditure, and maintenance budgets. Leading and supporting maintenance staff to achieve operational goals. Identifying and implementing improvements that enhance asset performance and maintenance outcomes. Working across a diverse campus portfolio, you'll combine your technical building expertise with project coordination, financial management, and people leadership skills to support the ongoing stewardship of the University's physical assets. Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This permanent role is based at our Lincoln Campus and works 40 hours per week (1.0 FTE). The salary for this position is circa $100,000 per annum, depending on experience. Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role To be successful in the role you will have: A trade qualification in carpentry, with an LBP qualification preferred. Significant carpentry experience, ideally with exposure to planned and preventative maintenance programmes. Experience coordinating or managing building maintenance or construction projects. Strong organisational skills with the ability to plan, prioritise, and manage multiple workstreams. A practical, cost-conscious approach to maintenance and project delivery. Excellent relationship-building skills and the ability to work effectively with a wide range of stakeholders. A proactive commitment to health and safety best practice. Leadership capability, flexibility, and the ability to adapt to changing priorities. Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Generous leave entitlements – six weeks of paid leave per year (based on 1.0 FTE) Annual salary reviews to recognise your contribution Contributions toward eye tests and prescription eyewear Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to Courtney.Ritter@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-25659 Posted on 29 Sep 2026 Closes on 14 Oct 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Facilities Management Work type(s) Permanent full-time More details (document) PD Building Maintenance Lead September 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 9 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
